<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solidarity (Polish trade union)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Solidarność</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solidarity is a Polish trade union that emerged at the Lenin Shipyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> in August 1980, the first independent union in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> country to be recognised by the state. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>, it had 10 million members at its peak in September 1981, a third of the working-age population. The government tried to destroy it with martial law from December 1981, but it survived underground. In the elections agreed at the Round Table talks of 1989 it won 99 of the 100 Senate seats, and a Solidarity-led government followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union.jpg" alt="Solidarity (Polish trade union)" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zenon Mirota · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strajk_sierpniowy_w_Stoczni_Gda%C5%84skiej_im._Lenina_34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1980 a strike at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk won the right to form independent unions; <b>Solidarity</b>, founded that September as the first independent union recognised in the Soviet bloc, soon had about ten million members.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height <b>Solidarity</b> had over 10 million members, the largest union membership in the world, and the philosopher Leszek Kołakowski called it a working-class revolution “directed against a socialist state, and carried out under the sign of the cross, with the blessing of the Pope.”</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Manmohan Singh recited its first line in his Budget Speech of 1992 in the Lok Sabha, and in recent years it has been taken up by mass movements such as the anti-CAA protests in India and the Pakistani Students <b>Solidarity</b> March.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Solidaritätslied&quot; (<b>Solidarity</b> Song) is a revolutionary workers' song written between 1929 and 1931 by Bertolt Brecht and set to music by Hanns Eisler.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied</a></li><li><blockquote>Chaplin was a committed member of the Industrial Workers of the World, the &quot;Wobblies&quot;, and wrote for <b>Solidarity</b>, the union's paper in the eastern United States, and drew its cartoons.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Solidarity</b> won all but one of the freely contested seats.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
